          "english_quote": "“Renewed our life” follows the printed form compared with Latin Vulgata and Palatina; the apparatus reports an Athos form meaning “renewed” with different grammar. Renewing the spirit and renewing life remain distinct expressions.",
          "issue": "The supplied apparatus transcription reports ἀνένευσε A, not another grammatical form straightforwardly meaning “renewed.” As written, ἀνένευσε belongs to ἀνανεύω, associated with nodding or a gesture of refusal. The note therefore mischaracterizes the reported alternative by asserting semantic agreement and merely different grammar. The supplied evidence does not establish that this is an orthographic corruption intended to mean “renewed.” This does not invalidate the selected working reading ἀνενέωσε.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain “renewed our life” in the main text. Revise the note to disclose the exact reported form and the interpretive limit, for example: “The printed working text reads ἀνενέωσε, ‘renewed,’ with Latin Vulgata and Palatina cited in the apparatus. The apparatus reports Athos ἀνένευσε, a different form whose intended sense here is uncertain; the supplied report does not establish it as another grammatical form meaning ‘renewed.’ Renewing the spirit and renewing life remain distinct expressions.” Any stronger explanation of the Athos form requires additional attributed evidence. The proposed note requires fresh review.",
